s a continuous tension  and core rigidity  this is crucial as mentioned for  building resilience against injury  at the same time the lizard crawl  involves a rotational element as you'll  often have your weight on just one arm  and be forced to combat those rotational  forces  has all kinds of additional benefits too  like improved hip mobility  and because you can do it for distance  or for time it's ideal for endurance  and it's also just a lot of fun  handstands  the handstand is a surprisingly useful  tool for training the core  as much as anything this teaches greater  awareness of the core as you need to  keep it aligned  to avoid it toppling over whilst you're  upside down and everything's topsy-turvy  it actually becomes an anti-extension  movement in some ways as keeping your  core rigid allows you to lean slightly  forwards and then use your fingers to  hold yourself up  one armed shoulder press performing any  type of shoulder press with one arm  instead of two  will allow you to build core stability  as you fight the forces trying to bend  you to one side  there are other great advantages to this  kind of unilateral training as well  so i highly recommend that every now and  then you half the weight and remove one  hand  loaded carries i've talked about these a  lot but loaded carriers are so  beneficial that it bears repeating  again these are so valuable because  we're keeping the core braced for long  periods to build strength endurance  this is exactly the kind of scenario we  face in real life you can use loaded  carries with a heavy trap bar  by holding something over your shoulders  or by holding a weight in just one hand  called a suitcase carry and this again  adds that anti-lateral flexion element  similar to a side plank or a one-armed  shoulder press  put these at the end of your workout to  avoid fatiguing your core prior to heavy  lifts that could leave you susceptible  to injury  quasi-isometric push-up performed  correctly high rep push-ups should have  all the same benefits as the plank  as they force you to maintain a rigid  core even as you grow close to fatigue  problem is that a lot of people won't be  able to perform push-ups for two minutes  straight  or forget about their posture and let  their mid-sections sag  a solution is to use the quasi-isometric  push-up an extremely slow push-up  lasting a minute or more lets you focus  on keeping the core solid  whilst also providing countless other  benefits to add an anti-rotational  element  try performing these on just one hand  front squat goblet squat  so many programs designed to build core  stability focus on anti-extension  when actually a lot of injuries come  from flexion many of us spend most of  our day in flexion after all  and regularly need to lift heavy things  off the floor in front of us  of course sit-ups will help you to build  strength here but not strength endurance  performing squats with the weight in  front of you then using a sandbag a  barbell or  zercher carry for instance will allow  you to build that endurance and  use that core strength to keep you  upright even when there's a weight  pulling you  back down the other way medicine ball  slam the medicine ball slam involves  throwing the ball down from a fully  extended position  that starting position mimics the way we  often use our core in real life from a  stretched out  extension and it means that you're using  a fuller range of motion that can lead  to awesome and more impressive  hypertrophy  leg raises and frog kicks hanging leg  raises are amazing for training the  rectus abdomini as long as you make sure  to curl the stomach  rather than simply hinging at the hips  which trains the hip flexors  rather than the core add weight between  your legs and you'll provide the abs  with the resistance that is often  lacking in this kind of training  v-ups v-ups are an explosive alternative  to the sit-up or crunch that increase  the challenge and help you to generate  